{
 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "core",
   "jp": "基本",
   "en": "The three cores",
   "blurb": "Almost every simple sentence is one of these three: something is something, something exists, or something has a quality. Get the particles right here and the rest follows."
  },
  {
   "id": "doing",
   "jp": "動作",
   "en": "Doing something",
   "blurb": "The verb goes last, and the slots in front of it can be reordered freely. Each particle says what its slot is doing, so the order is a matter of emphasis, not grammar."
  },
  {
   "id": "tense",
   "jp": "時制",
   "en": "Tense and politeness",
   "blurb": "Japanese has two tenses, not three: past and non-past. The non-past covers now and the future, and the context tells you which."
  },
  {
   "id": "question",
   "jp": "疑問",
   "en": "Questions",
   "blurb": "A question is a statement plus か. The question word goes in the slot you are asking about, and everything else stays where it was."
  },
  {
   "id": "modal",
   "jp": "助動詞",
   "en": "Wanting, being able, having to",
   "blurb": "These attach to the verb and change what the sentence claims: that you want it, can do it, must do it, or may do it."
  },
  {
   "id": "join",
   "jp": "接続",
   "en": "Joining two clauses",
   "blurb": "Japanese joins clauses at the end of the first one. The join tells you the relationship: and, because, when, if, even though."
  },
  {
   "id": "particles",
   "jp": "助詞",
   "en": "Particle clinic",
   "blurb": "The pairs that cause most of the mistakes, side by side."
  }
 ],
